    body: `
<p>Family is where Telugu is most precise. English "brother" and "sister" are not enough, and respect shows up in the verb. Heritage learners: these are the words your grandparents will listen for first.</p>
<h3>My, your, our</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Word</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td class="k">నా</td><td>my</td></tr><tr><td class="k">నీ</td><td>your (to a friend)</td></tr><tr><td class="k">మీ</td><td>your (polite, or to several people)</td></tr><tr><td class="k">మా</td><td>our (not including you)</td></tr><tr><td class="k">మన</td><td>our (including you)</td></tr></table></div>
<p>With family and home, Telugu speakers usually say <b>మా</b> <i>mā</i> where English says "my": <b>మా అమ్మ</b> <i>mā amma</i> is "my mother", literally "our mother". <b>నా</b> <i>nā</i> is also heard. Use <b>మన</b> <i>mana</i> only when the listener shares it: <b>మన ఊరు</b> <i>mana ūru</i> to someone from your own town.</p>
{{ex:మా అమ్మ|mā amma|my mother}}
<h3>Older or younger</h3>
<p>Brothers and sisters always come with their age:</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th></th><th>older</th><th>younger</th></tr><tr><td>brother</td><td class="k">అన్న</td><td class="k">తమ్ముడు</td></tr><tr><td>sister</td><td class="k">అక్క</td><td class="k">చెల్లెలు</td></tr></table></div>
<p>Parents are <b>అమ్మ</b> <i>amma</i> and <b>నాన్న</b> <i>nānna</i>. Grandmother depends on the side: <b>అమ్మమ్మ</b> <i>ammamma</i> (mother's mother), <b>నాన్నమ్మ</b> <i>nānnamma</i> (father's mother); grandfather is <b>తాతయ్య</b> <i>tātayya</i>. A son is <b>కొడుకు</b> <i>koḍuku</i>, a daughter <b>కూతురు</b> <i>kūturu</i>, and children <b>పిల్లలు</b> <i>pillalu</i>. You will also hear <b>అన్నా</b> <i>annā</i> and <b>అక్కా</b> <i>akkā</i> used to address a man or a woman a little older than you, such as a shopkeeper or a driver: it is friendly, not a claim of family. To introduce someone, use <b>ఇతను</b> <i>itanu</i> (this man) or <b>ఈమె</b> <i>īme</i> (this woman), the "this" partners of అతను and ఆమె.</p>
{{ex:ఇతను మా తమ్ముడు.|itanu mā tammuḍu.|This is my younger brother.}}
{{ex:ఈమె మా అక్క.|īme mā akka.|This is my older sister.}}
<h3>Respect: గారు and the plural verb</h3>
<p>Parents, grandparents and other elders often get <b>గారు</b> <i>gāru</i>, and then the verb takes the respect plural <b>-ఆరు</b>, the same ending as <b>మీరు</b> <i>mīru</i>. A younger brother takes the masculine <b>-ఆడు</b>, a younger sister the non-masculine form. The verbs <b>ఉన్నారు</b> <i>unnāru</i> (from "How are you?") and <b>వచ్చారు</b> <i>vacchāru</i> ("has come") show it:</p>
{{ex:మీ నాన్న గారు ఎలా ఉన్నారు?|mī nānna gāru elā unnāru?|How is your father?}}
{{ex:మీ తమ్ముడు ఎలా ఉన్నాడు?|mī tammuḍu elā unnāḍu?|How is your younger brother?}}
{{ex:మా నాన్న గారు వచ్చారు.|mā nānna gāru vacchāru.|My father has come.}}
<h3>Counting people</h3>
<p>As in lesson 10, people take the human counting form: <b>ఇద్దరు</b> <i>iddaru</i>, <b>ముగ్గురు</b> <i>mugguru</i>, <b>నలుగురు</b> <i>naluguru</i>. With the నాకు-construction, a short list needs no verb at all:</p>
{{ex:మాకు ఇద్దరు పిల్లలు.|māku iddaru pillalu.|We have two children.}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> forgetting the respect plural after గారు. <b>మా నాన్న గారు వచ్చాడు</b> → <b>మా నాన్న గారు వచ్చారు</b>.</div>
`,
