    deck: "Time, days and routine",
    body: `
<p>English has "I go" for habits and "I will go" for plans. Telugu uses one form for both: <b>వెళ్తాను</b> <i>veḷtānu</i> means "I go" and "I'll go". Words like <b>రోజూ</b> <i>rōjū</i> (every day) and <b>రేపు</b> <i>rēpu</i> (tomorrow) make it clear. Telugu uses this form far more than English uses "will": where you live, what you eat, when you get up all come in this form. The romanisation is a crutch: read the Telugu first.</p>
<h3>One form, many persons</h3>
<p>The form has <b>-తా-</b> (or <b>-తు-</b>) in the middle and the person ending at the end. The ending must agree with the subject:</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Who</th><th>do, will do</th><th>don't, won't</th></tr><tr><td class="k">నేను</td><td class="k">చేస్తాను</td><td class="k">చేయను</td></tr><tr><td class="k">నువ్వు</td><td class="k">చేస్తావు</td><td class="k">చేయవు</td></tr><tr><td class="k">మీరు, people</td><td class="k">చేస్తారు</td><td class="k">చేయరు</td></tr><tr><td class="k">అతను</td><td class="k">చేస్తాడు</td><td class="k">చేయడు</td></tr><tr><td class="k">ఆమె, అది</td><td class="k">చేస్తుంది</td><td class="k">చేయదు</td></tr><tr><td class="k">మేము, మనం</td><td class="k">చేస్తాం</td><td class="k">చేయం</td></tr></table></div>
<p><b>చేస్తాము</b> <i>chēstāmu</i> is also correct; <b>చేస్తాం</b> <i>chēstām</i> is the everyday form. As always, <b>ఆమె</b> <i>āme</i> takes the same form as <b>అది</b> <i>adi</i>.</p>
{{ex:నేను రోజూ ఆఫీసుకి వెళ్తాను.|nēnu rōjū āfīsuki veḷtānu.|I go to the office every day.}}
{{ex:నేను రేపు వస్తాను.|nēnu rēpu vastānu.|I'll come tomorrow.}}
{{ex:ఆమె హైదరాబాదులో ఉంటుంది.|āme haidarābādulō uṇṭundi.|She lives in Hyderabad.}}
<h3>Verbs to know</h3>
<p>Learn the "I" form of each, and the others follow: <b>వెళ్తాను</b> (go), <b>వస్తాను</b> <i>vastānu</i> (come), <b>తింటాను</b> <i>tiṇṭānu</i> (eat), <b>తాగుతాను</b> <i>tāgutānu</i> (drink), <b>ఉంటాను</b> <i>uṇṭānu</i> (stay, live), <b>చదువుతాను</b> <i>chaduvutānu</i> (read, study), <b>పని చేస్తాను</b> <i>pani chēstānu</i> (work).</p>
{{ex:మీరు ఏం తాగుతారు?|mīru ēm tāgutāru?|What will you have to drink?}}
<p>Some bases change before <b>-తా-</b>: <b>తిను</b> <i>tinu</i> (eat) gives <b>తింటాను</b>, and <b>రా</b> <i>rā</i> (come) gives <b>వస్తాను</b>. That is why the "I" form is the shortcut. Because the ending names the person, the pronoun is often left out: <b>రేపు వస్తాను</b> <i>rēpu vastānu</i> is a complete sentence. A host uses this form to offer, as above, and you use it to promise.</p>
<h3>Don't and won't: a form of its own</h3>
<p>English adds "don't" or "won't". Telugu drops the <b>-తా-</b> and puts the person ending straight on the verb's base: <b>చేయను</b> <i>chēyanu</i>, <b>వెళ్ళను</b> <i>veḷḷanu</i>, <b>తినను</b> <i>tinanu</i>, <b>తాగను</b> <i>tāganu</i>, and <b>రాను</b> <i>rānu</i> from "come". No <b>లేదు</b> <i>lēdu</i> is added.</p>
{{ex:నేను మాంసం తినను.|nēnu māmsam tinanu.|I don't eat meat.}}
{{ex:అతను రేపు రాడు.|atanu rēpu rāḍu.|He won't come tomorrow.}}
<p>Listen for vowel length. <b>వెళ్ళను</b>, "I won't go", has a short a. In lesson 19 you will meet <b>వెళ్ళాను</b> <i>veḷḷānu</i>, with a long ā: it means "I went".</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> adding లేదు for "won't". <b>నేను వెళ్తాను లేదు</b> → <b>నేను వెళ్ళను</b>. And keep the ending in agreement: <b>ఆమె వెళ్తాడు</b> → <b>ఆమె వెళ్తుంది</b>.</div>
`,
